  "summary": "Warp-faced Persian brocade of Yazd, dense with paisley boteh rows and gold-wrapped silk.",
  "description": "Termeh is a luxurious handwoven cloth of Yazd: fine silk warps crossed by densely beaten wefts so the surface shimmers with rows of tiny boteh paisleys, cypress trees and arabesques, often with gold- or silver-wrapped thread. Traditionally cut into ceremonial cloths, trousseau pieces, sanctuary coverings and elite robes, the weave is slow — a day's work may advance centimetres. Machine competition thinned the workshops, but a handful of Yazd looms keep the craft alive as heritage textile.",
